  "source": {
    "post_stream": {
      "posts": [
        {
          "id": 1916837,
          "username": "Gaurav_Trenoxahq",
          "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/community.shopify.com/gaurav_trenoxahq/{size}/416151_2.png",
          "created_at": "2026-10-17T09:41:55.073Z",
          "cooked": "<p>For those selling wholesale or B2B on Shopify: how do you catch a regular buyer who has quietly stopped ordering?</p>\n<p>Shopify B2B covers companies, catalogs and payment terms, but I haven’t found anything built in that flags “this account usually orders every 3 weeks and it’s been 7”. What I mostly see is someone exporting orders now and then, sorting by last order date and emailing whoever looks late, which tends to slip on busy weeks.</p>\n<p>Disclosure: I build n8n automations for Shopify B2B, and while testing a reorder reminder two things surprised me:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A fixed rule like “remind after 30 days” fits almost nobody. Buyers have very different rhythms, so using each buyer’s own typical gap between past orders worked much better.</li>\n<li>Buyers whose last order hasn’t shipped yet should be skipped. A “time to reorder?” email while they’re still waiting for the last delivery looks careless.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Curious how others handle it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Do you track reorder timing per buyer, or just by a fixed number of days?</li>\n<li>Does the follow-up come from sales personally, or an automated email?</li>\n<li>Has anyone found that a pre-filled “reorder the same items” link actually gets used by B2B buyers?</li>\n</ol>",
          "post_number": 1,
          "post_type": 1,
          "posts_count": 2,
          "updated_at": "2026-10-17T09:41:55.073Z",
          "reply_count": 0,
          "reply_to_post_number": null,
          "quote_count": 0,
