        {
          "id": 1917043,
          "username": "BobCSV",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/b/977dab/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-10-18T04:25:33.301Z",
          "cooked": "<p>Per buyer, for me — a fixed 30 days creates both false alarms and missed accounts, for exactly the reason you found.</p>\n<p>The low-tech version that doesn’t slip on busy weeks is a weekly order export turned into one row per company:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>last order date, and whether that order is actually fulfilled</li>\n<li>typical gap between orders — I’d use the median gap, not the average, once a buyer has 3+ orders, because one holiday order throws the average off</li>\n<li>expected next order = last order date + that typical gap, flagging only when today is past expected plus a grace of roughly a quarter of their usual gap</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then sort the flagged list by days overdue, weighted by usual order value, so on a busy week you only have to work the top handful. Buyers with fewer than 3 orders fall back to a segment default until they have a rhythm of their own. And yes, skip anyone whose last order hasn’t shipped — I’d hold the flag until it’s fulfilled, not just placed.</p>\n<p>On personal vs automated: hybrid works best. Let the automation (or the sheet) build the list, but have sales send the first nudge to the accounts that matter — two lines naming what they last ordered lands very differently from a generic reminder. Save the fully automated email for the long tail.</p>\n<p>The pre-filled reorder link does get used, in my experience, when the basket is editable and the items and prices are still current. It’s worth checking before you send, though — a link that opens on a discontinued product or old catalog pricing does more harm than no link.</p>\n<p>This kind of per-company cadence sheet is exactly the sort of one-off spreadsheet cleanup that gets posted on <a href=\"http://runeloresupply.com\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">runeloresupply.com</a> — someone turns the messy order export into a sheet you can just refresh each week instead of rebuilding the sort from scratch. And if you want to compare notes on these CSV/ops workflows, a few of us do that in a small Slack: <a href=\"https://join.slack.com/t/runelore/shared_invite/zt-4d8gp8llf-gtKtgzwsV5bdF6NoYZysOg\" class=\"inline-onebox\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">Join RuneLore on Slack</a> — you’d be welcome.</p>",
