      "id": 1843158,
      "summarized_text": "The discussion focuses on identifying Shopify B2B buyers who have stopped reordering without relying on a fixed reminder interval or manual order checks. The suggested approach is to track each company’s ordering cadence, since buyers’ schedules vary.\n\nA practical weekly workflow is to export orders and calculate each buyer’s last order date, fulfillment status, and typical time between orders. For buyers with at least three orders, use the median gap to reduce the effect of unusual orders; add a grace period of about one-quarter of their usual interval before flagging them. Newer buyers can use a segment-wide default. Prioritize overdue accounts by how late they are and their usual order value, and do not flag an order until it has shipped.\n\nFollow-up can be hybrid: sales should personally contact important accounts, while automated messages can serve lower-priority buyers. Pre-filled reorder links may help if buyers can edit the basket and the products and prices are current. No implementation decision or definitive resolution is reported; the discussion offers operational recommendations, but leaves the effectiveness of these methods open.",
